WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:05.005
Hey! Oh, no, I can't
 I can't hear you, Haley, at all.

00:00:05.005 --> 00:00:05.964
Unmute.

00:00:05.964 --> 00:00:07.507
Yeah.

00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:09.217
Hello, hello, hello.

00:00:09.217 --> 00:00:10.385
Nice to hear you.

00:00:10.385 --> 00:00:11.136
 Hello!

00:00:11.136 --> 00:00:12.595
So nice to meet you. 

00:00:15.682 --> 00:00:18.184
I am Rawson Marshall Thurber,
 and I'm here talking with

00:00:18.184 --> 00:00:21.021
Haley Hope Bartels, who just
won the Nicholl Fellowship

00:00:21.021 --> 00:00:23.064
 for her fantastic script
Pumping Black.

00:00:23.064 --> 00:00:26.026
it is about an aging
cyclist in the 1990s

00:00:26.026 --> 00:00:27.694
who, when threatened
with being cut from

00:00:27.694 --> 00:00:29.863
his team right before
the Tour de France,

00:00:29.863 --> 00:00:32.323
takes up a dangerous offer
 from a team doctor

00:00:32.323 --> 00:00:33.908
to engage in doping.

00:00:33.908 --> 00:00:36.494
Congratulations on
Pumping Black and on being 

00:00:36.494 --> 00:00:38.413
being a Nicholls Fellow

00:00:38.413 --> 00:00:39.748
I am-

00:00:39.748 --> 00:00:43.043
Every morning I wake up
 and I'm waiting for someone to be like,

00:00:43.293 --> 00:00:46.629
there's been a huge mistake. 

00:00:46.629 --> 00:00:49.174
But it's so far, it's held so far. So- 

00:00:49.174 --> 00:00:52.802
Ah yeah, it's not a very
elaborate episode of Punk’d.

00:00:52.802 --> 00:00:54.095
Yeah.

00:00:54.095 --> 00:00:56.097
So tell me a little bit
about submitting

00:00:56.097 --> 00:00:58.058
and what that
process was like.

00:00:58.058 --> 00:01:02.187
Yeah, so I graduated AFI in 2019.

00:01:02.187 --> 00:01:04.606
A couple months after that,
 the pandemic hit.

00:01:04.606 --> 00:01:05.774
 Perfect.

00:01:05.815 --> 00:01:10.570
and it was mostly
like a sea of no's.

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:13.948
Applying to the Nicholl this year,
I almost didn’t do it.

00:01:13.948 --> 00:01:18.161
Because I was like, “I keep sending
things to competitions and

00:01:18.161 --> 00:01:20.413
people keep saying no.”

00:01:20.413 --> 00:01:22.999
I asked my boyfriend, like,
 “Should I apply that?

00:01:22.999 --> 00:01:24.375
Should he even apply
to the Nicholl this year?” 

00:01:24.375 --> 00:01:25.835
He’s like “Yes, you have to do it.”

00:01:25.835 --> 00:01:28.338
And I was like, “But they're not
 going to like it like they never like it.”

00:01:28.338 --> 00:01:30.548
He's like, “You have to apply.
 You just have to do it.”

00:01:30.548 --> 00:01:31.466
Good boyfriend.

00:01:31.466 --> 00:01:33.426
What do you think it was
about Pumping Black

00:01:33.426 --> 00:01:35.261
 that the deciders liked that

00:01:35.261 --> 00:01:37.347
they maybe didn't see
 in your previous writing? 

00:01:37.347 --> 00:01:40.975
This script is much more
 specific and weird, I think.

00:01:40.975 --> 00:01:44.896
Even though it's  a very
fantastical, sort of specific

00:01:44.896 --> 00:01:48.942
world of professional cycling
 in which I have never personally lived

00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:54.864
the element of perfectionism
 and obsession, I think that came out

00:01:55.824 --> 00:01:58.409
in this script in a way
 that it hasn’t in my other writing.

00:01:58.743 --> 00:02:00.120
It's a whole world of

00:02:01.037 --> 00:02:03.373
doping and racing in
the Tour de France,

00:02:03.373 --> 00:02:07.001
but there's not much of a mention
 of the one and only Lance Armstrong.

00:02:07.127 --> 00:02:08.878
That's very intentional. Yeah.

00:02:08.878 --> 00:02:09.295
Yeah.

00:02:09.295 --> 00:02:13.133
The story of Lance Armstrong
 is the doping story that everybody knows.

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:15.677
Maybe people know Barry Bonds.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.303
I'm a Giants fan like-

00:02:17.303 --> 00:02:18.138
Me too.

00:02:18.388 --> 00:02:20.348
Oh, how are you doing?

00:02:20.348 --> 00:02:21.641
Not great. Not great.

00:02:21.641 --> 00:02:22.433
Yeah, me neither

00:02:22.433 --> 00:02:24.978
Well, you know, it's a it's
 a really powerful screenplay

00:02:25.562 --> 00:02:28.690
it's all really, really juicy stuff
 and beautifully rendered in your work.

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:31.067
So I'm not surprised in the least 

00:02:31.067 --> 00:02:33.153
that you were selected
so congratulations.

00:02:33.153 --> 00:02:34.612
For you and your writing,

00:02:34.612 --> 00:02:38.908
what would you find is the most
 challenging part of it for you?

00:02:39.325 --> 00:02:40.493
Is it the beginning? 

00:02:40.493 --> 00:02:43.496
Is it finishing or
 do you get mired in the middle?

00:02:43.496 --> 00:02:45.498
I'm so happy you asked. 

00:02:45.498 --> 00:02:49.752
We have a big, huge like
eight-by-five whiteboard 

00:02:49.752 --> 00:02:51.171
and I put my nine beats up

00:02:51.171 --> 00:02:53.965
or if I'm doing a pilot
 and that is the most difficult part.

00:02:54.007 --> 00:02:55.592
And once I've done that for

00:02:56.718 --> 00:02:58.928
two, three, six months, then I can

00:02:58.928 --> 00:03:00.096
write a scripts in a week.

00:03:00.096 --> 00:03:02.724
 Like pumping black,
 like first draft for it in a week.

00:03:02.932 --> 00:03:04.976
It took me six months
 to write it in a week.

00:03:05.393 --> 00:03:09.981
I would love your advice
 on, you know, as somebody

00:03:09.981 --> 00:03:12.734
who's been very successful
in the industry

00:03:12.734 --> 00:03:14.944
since at least 2006,

00:03:16.196 --> 00:03:18.364
Dodgeball is awesome, by the way. 

00:03:18.364 --> 00:03:20.617
My dad still quotes 
that movie all the time.

00:03:20.617 --> 00:03:23.578
Tell you your dad,
I'm a big fan of his.

00:03:23.578 --> 00:03:24.704
He sounds like a very smart man. 

00:03:24.704 --> 00:03:26.664
Yeah, he's a genius 

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:28.416
That sounded sarcastic, but he is. 

00:03:28.416 --> 00:03:29.459
He is a very smart man. 

00:03:29.459 --> 00:03:33.379
Success in this town and maybe success
 and any competitive field,

00:03:33.379 --> 00:03:35.465
you know, really comes
down to three things

00:03:35.465 --> 00:03:38.301
as far as I have been
able to suss out.

00:03:38.301 --> 00:03:41.846
And it's hard work, luck and talent.

00:03:42.013 --> 00:03:44.974
You put in the hours, you know,
your butt in the seat

00:03:44.974 --> 00:03:47.227
 working on, you know,
on your screenplay. 

00:03:47.227 --> 00:03:50.480
And then I found that the harder
 I work, the luckier I've gotten.

00:03:50.647 --> 00:03:52.941
You could have not
written Pumping Black.

00:03:52.941 --> 00:03:54.734
You could have not submitted it,

00:03:54.734 --> 00:03:57.445
had your boyfriend not
made you do it.

00:03:57.445 --> 00:03:58.488
But you could have not, right? 

00:03:58.488 --> 00:04:01.783
Like you could have said, “I'm tired
 of being passed on and I'm going to go”

00:04:01.783 --> 00:04:03.284
You know, but you didn't. 

00:04:03.284 --> 00:04:06.120
And thank goodness
 for that, right?

00:04:06.162 --> 00:04:09.040
What are you doing next?
 What's the plan?

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:12.252
Yeah. Well, I’ve got to write a script
 for the Nicoll Committee

00:04:12.252 --> 00:04:13.002
over the next year.

00:04:13.002 --> 00:04:13.628
Oh yeah?

00:04:13.628 --> 00:04:14.754
Yeah, I’ve got to do that. 

00:04:14.754 --> 00:04:16.547
I haven't decided
 what that's going to be yet. 

00:04:16.547 --> 00:04:18.758
I wish you all the best
 and congratulations

00:04:19.008 --> 00:04:21.761
on winning the Nicholl
 and I'm stoked for you.

00:04:21.761 --> 00:04:22.428
 And good luck.

00:04:22.512 --> 00:04:26.557
Truly, like this has been so delightful
 and I had the best time.

00:04:26.599 --> 00:04:27.767
Thank you again. 

00:04:27.767 --> 00:04:28.559
Thank you. Thank you. 

00:04:28.559 --> 00:04:29.227
And go Giants!

00:04:29.227 --> 00:04:30.228
Giants.

00:04:30.228 --> 00:04:31.396
Giants. Next year.

00:04:31.604 --> 00:04:32.272
We're going to get it.

00:04:32.272 --> 00:04:33.439
 Yes. Yes, ma'am. See you later.

